# QUESTION : RATE LIMITING KYA HAI ?
======================================

Rate Limiting ek mechanism hai jo kisi client ko ek defined time window me 
limited number of requests hi bhejne deta hai, limit cross hote hi request 
reject ho jaati hai (usually 429 Too Many Requests).

--------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION : RATE LIMITING  KITNE TARIKON SE LAGA SAKTE HO ?
--------------------------------------------------------------

1. IP-BASED RATE LIMITING
---------------------------

Example : 5 req/min per IP

Kisi bhi IP address se 1 minute ke window me maximum 5 requests allowed hain.


### QUESTION : IP-BASED RATE LIMITING KAB USE KARTE HAIN ?
-------------------------------------------------------------

IP-based rate limiting un endpoints ke liye use karte hain jahan user ka authenticated identity available nahi hota —
matlab humare paas user ko uniquely identify karne ke liye koi authenticated identifier (jaise user_id) nahi hota.
Aise endpoints hote hain — Login, Register, Forgot Password, OTP Send, ya public/anonymous APIs.


### QUESTION : IP-BASED RATE LIMITING KI LIMITATION ?
--------------------------------------------------------

#### QUESTION: PAT KYA HAI?
------------------------------

- Jab koi device — laptop, mobile ya TV — internet access karna chahti
  hai, toh sabse pehle woh router se connect hoti hai.

- Router device ko ek private IP address deta hai, jaise '192.168.1.5'.

- Private IP sirf local network par valid hota hai. Internet par valid nahi hota.
  Internet par hosted resources ko access karne ke liye public IP chahiye.

- Device ke andar OS network connection ke liye ek source port bhi
  assign karta hai, jaise '53000'.

- Jab device internet par request bhejti hai, router uske private IP aur
  private port ko apne public IP (jo ISP router ko provide karta hai,
  jaise '49.205.10.20') aur public port mein translate karta hai. Isi
  process ko PAT (Port Address Translation) kehte hain.

- Router different connections/devices ko uniquely identify karne ke
  liye different public ports use karta hai. Ye public ports wo khud
  assign karta hai.

- Agar router sirf different private IPs ko same public IP me convert kar
  de aur port translation na kare, to jab destination se response aayega,
  router different devices/connections ko uniquely identify nahi kar payega
  kyunki sabhi devices ka same public IP hoga. Port translation isi problem
  ko solve karta hai. Isi wajah se ghar ya office ke multiple devices same 
  public IP share karke internet access kar paate hain, bina kisi confusion ke.


Example :
 
- Ghar/office mein 3 devices — teeno google.com access karna chahti hain:

    Laptop : 192.168.1.5:53000
    Phone  : 192.168.1.7:53000
    TV     : 192.168.1.10:53000

  Notice karo ki sabhi devices ka source port same hai, phir bhi ye uniquely 
  identifiable hain kyunki unke private IP addresses different hain. Network 
  connections ko IP address + port ke combination se identify kiya jaata hai.

- Router ka public IP: 49.205.10.20

- Router PAT perform karta hai:

    192.168.1.5:53000   → 49.205.10.20:61000
    192.168.1.7:53000   → 49.205.10.20:61001
    192.168.1.10:53000  → 49.205.10.20:61002

  Public IP same (49.205.10.20), but public ports different
  isliye har device uniquely identify ho jaati hai.


- PAT Table:

    Public IP:Port        →  Private IP:Port
    --------------------------------------------------
    49.205.10.20:61000    →  192.168.1.5:53000   (Laptop)
    49.205.10.20:61001    →  192.168.1.7:53000   (Phone)
    49.205.10.20:61002    →  192.168.1.10:53000  (TV)


- Google ko teeno requests same public IP se dikhti hain, but ports
  different:

    49.205.10.20:61000 → Laptop ki connection
    49.205.10.20:61001 → Phone ki connection
    49.205.10.20:61002 → TV ki connection

- Google response bhejta hai (maan lo Laptop ke liye):

    49.205.10.20:61000

  Router PAT table check karta hai:

    49.205.10.20:61000 → 192.168.1.5:53000 → Laptop

Response Laptop ko forward ho jaata hai. ✅


#### IMPORTANT:
-----------------

NAT = Sirf IP translation
      Private IP → Public IP

PAT = IP + Port dono translate
      Private IP : Port → Public IP : Port


1: PAT Issue 
--------------

Agar hum IP-based rate limiting laga dein (i.e. public IP par), to ek user apna
limit cross karega, aur usi public IP ke peeche baithe baaki saare users bhi block
ho jaayenge — kyunki sabka public IP same hai. Is wajah se wo baaki users bhi
internet par hosted resources ko access nahi kar paayenge, chahe unhone kuch
galat nahi kiya.

Isi wajah se IP-based rate limiting ki limit thodi loose rakhte hain — taaki
PAT ke peeche baithe genuine users impact na hon.



2. IDENTIFIER BASED RATE LIMITING (Email / Mobile)
-----------------------------------------------------

Example:
  → 5 req/min per Email
  → 3 req/10min per Mobile

Kisi bhi email/mobile se 1 minute (ya 10 min) ke window me maximum
requests allowed hain — chahe request kisi bhi IP se aaye.


### IDENTIFIER-BASED LIMIT KI ZARURAT KYUN HAI ?
--------------------------------------------------

Agar identifier-based rate limiting na ho, to attacker bulk emails/mobiles
ka dump use karke different IPs se mass OTP requests bhej sakta hai —
aise endpoints pe jo OTP-based verification use karte hain.
Isse per-IP limit easily bypass ho jaati hai.

  - Company ka paisa waste — SMS / Email gateway cost (10,000 OTP = bada bill)
  - Victim user ko spam — phone me OTP messages ki barsaat

Isi ko OTP Flooding attack kehte hain.

Real-world: IRCTC, PhonePe, Paytm, WhatsApp — sabme OTP rate limiting hoti hai.


### QUESTION : KAHAN USE KARTE HAIN ?
---------------------------------------

Aise endpoints pe jahan user apna claimed identifier deta hai —
jaise Login, Register, Forgot Password, OTP Send.

(Claimed isliye, kyunki user ne khud bataya hai ki wo kaun hai.
Verify nahi hua hai.)

Generally identifier-based limit ko on top of IP-based limit lagate hain:

    /login
      → 5 req/min per IP               ← Layer 1
      → 5 req/min per Email/Mobile     ← Layer 2

Ab chahe attacker 100 different IPs use kare, agar wo same email/mobile
pe attack kar raha hai → identifier + IP based limit usse rok legi.


### QUESTION : LIMITATION ?
------------------------------

Agar attacker ke paas bulk emails/mobiles ka dump ho (data breach se),
to wo aise endpoints pe mass attack kar sakta hai — har request
different email/mobile aur different IP se bhej kar:

    Email-1  ← IP-1
    Mobile-2 ← IP-2
    Email-3  ← IP-3
    ...

To:
  - Per-IP limit FAIL        (har request new IP)
  - Per-Email limit FAIL     (har request new email)
  - Per-Mobile limit FAIL    (har request new mobile)
  - Saari layers bypass ho jaati hain


Solution : Global Rate Limit + CAPTCHA
----------------------------------------

Jab individual identifier limits bypass ho jaayein, tab aggregate
level pe rokna padta hai:

  - Global Rate Limit → Total requests/min cross (jaise 1000/min)
                        → CAPTCHA trigger
  - CAPTCHA           → Bots/scripts ruk jaate hain, attack stop




3. USER-BASED RATE LIMITING 
------------------------------

Example : /api/** → 100 requests/min per user

Ek logged-in user 1 minute me maximum 100 API calls kar sakta hai.


### QUESTION : KAHAN USE KARTE HAIN ?
---------------------------------------

Authenticated requests ke liye generally per-user rate limiting use karte hain.

User ko uniquely identify karne ke liye hum JWT token se 'sub' claim (user ID)
(verified identity - authentication ke baad mila, trusted hai) extract karte hain.
Ye user ki stable identity hai — same user ka same sub hota hai, chahe kitne bhi 
naye tokens issue hon.

Isse har user ki API request count independently track hoti hai aur
rate limit bhi individually enforce hoti hai.

Generally:

- Login/Register/OTP jaise unauthenticated endpoints pe hum
  IP + Email/Mobile combo rate limiting lagate hain.

- Login ke baad ke endpoints pe (yaani jo endpoints authenticated hain)
  hum USER-BASED RATE LIMITING lagate hain — kyunki wahan user ko uniquely
  identify karne wala authenticated attribute hamesha available hota hai,
  jaise user_id (JWT sub claim se).

Isliye user-based rate limiting "IP ka replacement" nahi hai —
wo "IP ka complement" hai. Dono different phases ke liye hain.


### BENIFIT:
--------------
User-based rate limiting NAT/PAT issue bhi solve kar deti hai —
jo IP-based rate limiting me hota hai, kyunki ek public IP ke peeche
multiple users ho sakte hain.



4. API KEY BASED RATE LIMITING 
-------------------------------

Example: 1000 req/hour per API key

1 hour me maximum 100 API calls kar sakta hai.


### QUESTION : KAHAN USE KARTE HAIN ?
---------------------------------------

Partner APIs, B2B integrations, aur signup-based public APIs ke liye.
Signup ke baad jab API key issue hoti hai, tab per API key limit lagate hain.
API key verified identity hai — signup ke through issue hoti hai, isliye trusted hai.
Attacker fake key nahi bhej sakta.


### BENIFIT:
--------------

1: IP-independent — PAT problem solve ho jaata hai.
   - Ek user apna limit cross kare → sirf uska API key block hoga.
     Baaki users apne API keys ke saath normally use kar sakte hain.

2: Revoke kar sakte ho agar abuse ho.


### NOTE:
---------

Truly anonymous API me na koi API key hoti hai, na hi user ka koi identity — sirf IP bacha hai.

Example: Public weather API.

Solution: Loose IP limit (1000/hour per IP).

Problem: PAT issue — NAT ke peeche ek user exceed kare to baaki sab users bhi block.
Isliye limit loose rakhni padti hai.

Perfect solution nahi hai.



5. Device-Based RATE LIMITING 
-------------------------------

Example: Device-abc-123 → 10 req/min

Device-abc-123 se 1 minute me maximum 10 API calls allowed hain.


### QUESTION : DEVICE ID KAISE GENERATE HOTI HAI ?
----------------------------------------------------

Device ID hardware me hardcoded nahi hoti — app khud generate karti hai 
pehli baar launch hone par, usse securely store kar leti hai, aur us 
device se jo bhi request jaati hai, uske header me wahi same Device ID 
bhejti hai.

Lekin banking apps sirf app-generated ID pe depend nahi karti — wo 
multiple signals combine karke device identity banati hain, jaise 
app-generated ID, Device Fingerprint (screen resolution, OS version, 
Hardware model), aur Behavior patterns (typing speed, tap patterns).

Kyunki ek source spoofable hota hai, but multiple signals ka combination 
spoof karna mushkil ho jaata hai.


#### IMPORTANT:
-----------------

Har app ki apni Device ID hoti hai. Ye globally unique nahi hoti.

SBI app    → Device ID: sbi-abc-123
HDFC app   → Device ID: hdfc-xyz-789
ICICI app  → Device ID: icici-pqr-456


### QUESTION : KAHAN USE KARTE HAIN ?
---------------------------------------

Mostly mobile apps me — particularly banking apps me.

Generally ise IP-based rate limiting ke upar ek additional layer ke 
roop me use karte hain.

- IP-based layer                     → DDoS aur basic abuse rokne ke liye
- Device-based layer                 → Same device se aane wale abuse ko rokta hai
- Identifier-based / User-based      → Same email/mobile ya same user_id pe 
                                        target attack ko rokta hai

Device-based aur Identifier-based — dono PAT problem solve karte hain, 
kyunki dono IP ko ignore karte hain. Dono parallel layers hain, ek 
doosre ka replacement nahi.


### EXAMPLE — OTP BOMBING ATTACK
---------------------------------

Agar attacker different devices (hence different Device IDs) aur 
different IPs (using VPN or device emulators) use karke same 
email/mobile pe OTP bombing kare, to:

- IP-based rate limiting bypass ho jaayegi (har request new IP se)
- Device-based rate limiting bhi bypass ho jaayegi (har request new 
  Device ID se)

Is case me Identifier-based / User-based rate limiting help karti hai — 
kyunki counter email/mobile ya user_id pe hota hai, IP ya Device ID pe 
nahi. Chahe attacker 100 different devices aur 100 different IPs use 
kare, same email/mobile pe limit cross hone par block ho jaayega.


### LIMITATION
-----------------

1: DEVICE ID SPOOFABLE HAI
------------------------------

Rooted device me app ka local storage attacker ke control me hota hai.
Device ID wahan store hoti hai, to attacker usse edit karke fresh 
Device ID set kar sakta hai — jisse device limit bypass ho jaayegi.

Isi wajah se production banking apps generally device-based rate 
limiting ke saath-saath user-based ya identifier-based rate limiting 
bhi lagate hain — taaki ek layer bypass ho bhi jaaye, to doosri 
layer rok sake.


2: DEVICE ID GLOBALLY UNIQUE NAHI HOTI
-------------------------------------------

Same device par har app ki apni Device ID hoti hai — globally unique 
nahi. SBI app, HDFC app, ICICI app — sabki alag.

Cross-app fraud detection iske through possible nahi.



### BENIFIT - PAT problem solve
--------------------------------

Ek public IP ke peeche multiple users ho sakte hain (office PAT, ghar ka WiFi).

IP-based me counter IP pe hota hai → ek user limit cross karega → usi IP ke 
peeche baithe baaki sab bhi block.

Device-based me counter Device ID pe hota hai → sirf wahi device block hoga, 
baaki normally use kar sakti hain.



---------------------------------------------
## QUESTION : RATE LIMITING KE ALGORITHMS ?
---------------------------------------------

1. FIXED WINDOW COUNTER
-------------------------

Fixed Window Counter me time ko fixed-size windows me toda jaata hai, aur har window ka 
apna independent counter hota hai. Jab bhi koi request aati hai, counter++ ho jaata hai. 
Jab counter limit cross karta hai, to request reject ho jaati hai. Aur jab nayi window 
start hoti hai, to counter reset ho jaata hai zero par.

Example :

Limit = 10,000 req/hr
Window = 1 hour

Window 1: 10:00 AM - 10:59 AM
  Counter starts at 0
  Request aayi → 1
  Request aayi → 2
  ...
  Request aayi → 10,000
  10,001st request → REJECT (429)

Window 2: 11:00 AM - 11:59 AM
  Counter reset → 0
  Fresh cycle



### QUESTION : DISTRIBUTED SYSTEM ME FIXED WINDOW COUNTER ALGORITHM KAISE KAAM KARTA HAI ?
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Client HTTP request bhejta hai, jo Load Balancer tak pahunchti hai.
   LB request ko kisi ek App Instance par route karta hai.


2. User jo bhi request bhejta hai, wo kisi na kisi window ka part hota hai.
   Aur jab user kisi window me pehli baar request bhejta hai, to 
   Application Instance us window ke liye ek key create karta hai 


   #### KEY FORMAT :
   ------------------

   ratelimit:entity:{entity_id}:window:{window_id}

   window_id = currentTime / windowSize (seconds me)


   #### EXAMPLE :
   ---------------

   100 requests per hour per user (user_id se identify)

   entity     = user
   entity_id  = user_id (jaise 101)
   window size =  1 hr (3600 sec.)

   Consider karo ki is window me user (user_id = 101) ki pehli request
   6:00 AM par aayi — yaani midnight (00:00:00) se 21,600 seconds baad.

   Reference point: 
   Day start = midnight (00:00:00) = 0 seconds
   6:00 AM = 21,600 seconds since midnight

   window_id  = 21,600 / 3600  = 6

   Key:
   ratelimit:user:101:window:6


   #### QUESTION : WINDOW KAISE DECIDE HOTI HAI ?
   -----------------------------------------------

   Window fixed hoti hai, request ke time se start nahi hoti.

   EXAMPLE:

     Requirement: 100 requests / hour
     First request aayi: 10:59 AM
     → Ye request 10:00 AM - 11:00 AM wali window me jaayegi
       (10:59 AM - 11:59 AM wali window me NAHI)


   STEP 1: Time ko seconds me convert karo (00:00:00 se ab tak)

     00:00:00  → 0 seconds
     01:00:00  → 3600 seconds
     .
     .
     .
     10:00:00  → 36000 sec
     10:59:00  → 39540 sec
     11:00:00  → 39600 sec


   STEP 2: Formula lagao

     window_id = currentTime / windowSize (integer division)

     10:59 AM pe:  window_id = 39540 / 3600 = 10.98 = 10
     10:58 AM pe:  window_id = 39480 / 3600 = 10.96 = 10  ← same
     11:00 AM pe:  window_id = 39600 / 3600 = 11.0  = 11  ← change


   STEP 3: window_id ka matlab

     window_id 0  → 00:00 - 00:59
     window_id 1  → 01:00 - 01:59
     ...
     window_id 10 → 10:00 - 10:59   ← 10:59 AM yahan
     window_id 11 → 11:00 - 11:59   ← 11:00 AM yahan

   Isliye 10:59 AM → window_id = 10 → 10:00-11:00 wali window


3. Ab App Instance is window ke liye jo key banayi hai, usse lekar
   Redis ke paas jaata hai, aur Redis ko INCR command bhejta hai.

       INCR ratelimit:user:101:window:6


4. Ab Redis ye dekhta hai:

   - Kya ye key (ratelimit:user:101:window:6) already exist karti hai?

   CASE 1: Key exist NAHI karti
   ------------------------------

   Matlab ye us window ki pehli request hai.
   Redis key create karta hai aur key ke corresponding counter jo
   conceptually 0 se start hota hai, usse 0 se 1 kar deta hai.
   Redis App Instance ko return karta hai: 1



   CASE 2: Key exist karti hai
   -----------------------------

   Matlab us window me pehle bhi requests aayi hain.
   Redis existing counter ki value ko +1 karta hai.
   Redis App Instance ko nayi incremented value return karta hai:
   (jaise 5 thi, to 6 return karega)


5. Agar wo us user ki us window me pehli request thi
   (yaani jab INCR ka result 1 aata hai), to Application 
   Instance us window ka TTL set kar deta hai:

    EXPIRE key 3600

   TTL = windowSize (jaise 100 req/hour ke liye windowSize = 1 hour).

   Subsequent requests pe EXPIRE nahi chalega — warna TTL baar-baar
   refresh hota rahega aur key kabhi expire hi nahi hogi.


6. App Instance ko Redis se jo count value mili, usse limit se compare
   karta hai.

   Counter ki maximum limit = no_of_requests_allowed
   (jaise requirement 100 req/hour hai to limit = 100).

       count <= 100  → ALLOW
       count >  100  → REJECT (429 Too Many Requests)


7. Allow hone par App Instance Database se actual data fetch karta hai,
   aur finally response client ko return ho jaata hai.
   Reject hone par Database touch bhi nahi hota.


8. Jaise-jaise window me nayi requests aati hain, counter badhta
   rehta hai. Jaise hi counter apni limit (no_of_requests_allowed)
   reach kar leta hai, us window me aane wali further requests reject
   ho jaati hain.


9. Jaise hi window ka TTL expire hota hai, matlab window complete ho
   gayi — us window ki key automatically Redis se delete ho jaati hai
   (pichle window ki key auto-cleanup).


10. Ab jab nayi window ke liye pehli request aati hai, to us nayi window
    ke liye ek nayi key create hoti hai, aur counter phir se fresh start
    karta hai (conceptually 0 se).



### QUESTION : LIMITATIONS KYA HAI ?
--------------------------------------

1: BOUNDARY BURST PROBLEM 
---------------------------

Boundary Burst Problem tab hota hai jab attacker window ke end aur next window ke start ko target karta hai.
Kyunki counter window change hone pe reset ho jaata hai, dono windows me limit independently allow ho jaati hai
jisse short time me 2x limit process ho jaati hai.

Iska seedha asar backend par padta hai. Agar tumhara backend 5,000 req/min handle karta hai, aur boundary burst 
ke through 1 minute me 20,000 requests aa gayi — to backend crash ho jaayega.


Example :

Limit: 10,000 requests per hour

WINDOW 1 (10:00 - 10:59):

10:00 - 10:59:30  → 0 requests (attacker wait kar raha hai)
10:59:30 - 10:59:59 → 10,000 requests (poora counter last 30 sec me khatam) → LIMIT FULL


WINDOW 2 (11:00 - 11:59):

11:00:00 → counter RESET → 0
11:00:00 - 11:00:29 → 10,000 requests (poora counter last 30 sec me khatam) → LIMIT FULL


Ab Dekho Timeline:

WINDOW 1: 
10:59:30 - 10:59:59 → 10,000 requests (30 sec me)

WINDOW 2: 
11:00:00 - 11:00:29 → 10,000 requests (30 sec me)

Total: 1 minute me 20,000 requests
Expected: 1 ghante me 10,000


2: DISTRIBUTED COUNTER INCONSISTENCY 
--------------------------------------

Distributed system me ek service ke multiple instances hote hain, aur unke aage ek Load Balancer hota hai
jo incoming requests ko un instances ke beech distribute karta hai. Agar har service instance apna local 
counter rakhega, to total limit cross ho jayegi — kyunki har instance apne counter ko independently track
karega, aur dusre instances ke counters ki usse koi jaankari nahi hogi.

Limit = 10,000 requests/hour
3 instances (LB ke peeche)

Instance-1 → 10,000 requests (apna counter full)
Instance-2 → 10,000 requests (apna counter full)
Instance-3 → 10,000 requests (apna counter full)

Total = 30,000 requests 🔥 (3x limit cross)


### SOLUTION : SHARED COUNTER (REDIS)
---------------------------------------

Isliye distributed system me is algorithm ko implement karne ke liye 
hum shared counter use karte hain — jo normally Redis ke through 
implement hota hai.



### QUESTION : KAHAN USE KARTE HAIN ?
--------------------------------------

Fixed Window generally billing aur daily limits ke liye best hai.
Critical endpoints pe nahi use karta kyunki boundary burst problem hai.

Example:

1. FREE TIER DAILY LIMITS
   Limit: 5 PDF downloads/day per user

2. MONTHLY API QUOTA (Stripe/AWS type)
   Limit: 10,000 calls/month per API key

3. PASSWORD RESET LIMIT
   Limit: 3 resets/day per user

4. DAILY EMAIL/SMS CAP
   Limit: 10,000 emails/day per campaign

5. INTERNAL SERVICE CALLS (Non-critical)
   Limit: 1000 calls/hour per service



2. SLIDING WINDOW LOG
-------------------------

Sliding Window me window FIXED nahi hoti. Har request ke saath 
window aage slide hoti jaati hai.

Sliding Window Log me hum har request ka timestamp store karte hain.
Isse implement karne ke liye Redis Sorted Set (ZSET) use karte hain.
Jab bhi nayi request aati hai, to current time ke hisaab se window slide 
hoti hai. Jo purane timestamps window se bahar ho gaye, unhe hata dete hain.
Iske baad hum dekhte hain ki ab new adjusted window me kitne total timestamps 
bache hue hain. Agar un timestamps ka count allowed count se kam hai 
(jaise agar limit hai 100 req/min, to allowed count = 100), to naye request ka 
timestamp store karte hain aur use allow karte hain. Warna reject kar dete hain 
(timestamp store nahi karte).

Matlab:

    Window = [current_time - windowSize, current_time]

Ye window HAR REQUEST par calculate hoti hai — current time ke hisaab se.


## KEY FORMAT :
-----------------

    ratelimit:{entity}:{entity_id}

    entity    = user / ip / mobile / apikey etc.
    entity_id = actual identifier (user_id, IP address, etc.)



## STEP BY STEP FLOW
------------------------

### EXAMPLE :
---------------

   Limit = 5 requests / 60 sec per user (user_id se identify)

   windowSize = 60 sec

   entity     = user
   entity_id  = user_id (jaise 101)

   Key:
   ratelimit:user:101

Har request ke time window apne aap slide hoti hai.
Window = [current_time - 60s, current_time]



### REQUEST 1 (user:101): 10:01:00
─────────────────────────────────────

  Window = [10:01:00 - 60s, 10:01:00]
         = [10:00:00, 10:01:00]

  Window ke andar timestamps (before add): []
  Count = 0 < 5 → ALLOW
  Naya timestamp add karo: 10:01:00

  Timestamps: [10:01:00]


### REQUEST 2 (user:101): 10:01:10
─────────────────────────────────────

  Window = [10:01:10 - 60s, 10:01:10]
         = [10:00:10, 10:01:10]

  Window ke andar timestamps: [10:01:00]
  Count = 1 < 5 → ALLOW
  Naya timestamp add karo: 10:01:10

  Timestamps: [10:01:00, 10:01:10]


### REQUEST 3 (user:101) : 10:01:20
─────────────────────────────────────

  Window = [10:01:20 - 60s, 10:01:20]
         = [10:00:20, 10:01:20]

  Window ke andar timestamps: [10:01:00, 10:01:10]
  Count = 2 < 5 → ALLOW
  Naya timestamp add karo: 10:01:20

  Timestamps: [10:01:00, 10:01:10, 10:01:20]


### REQUEST 4 (user:101) : 10:01:30
─────────────────────────────────────

  Window = [10:01:30 - 60s, 10:01:30]
         = [10:00:30, 10:01:30]

  Window ke andar timestamps: [10:01:00, 10:01:10, 10:01:20]
  Count = 3 < 5 → ALLOW
  Naya timestamp add karo: 10:01:30

  Timestamps: [10:01:00, 10:01:10, 10:01:20, 10:01:30]


### REQUEST 5 (user:101) : 10:01:40
─────────────────────────────────────

  Window = [10:01:40 - 60s, 10:01:40]
         = [10:00:40, 10:01:40]

  Window ke andar timestamps:
    [10:01:00, 10:01:10, 10:01:20, 10:01:30]
  Count = 4 < 5 → ALLOW
  Naya timestamp add karo: 10:01:40

  Timestamps: [10:01:00, 10:01:10, 10:01:20, 10:01:30, 10:01:40]
  Count = 5 (limit reached)


### REQUEST 6 (user:101) : 10:01:50   ⚠️ LIMIT CROSS
────────────────────────────────────

  Window = [10:01:50 - 60s, 10:01:50]
         = [10:00:50, 10:01:50]

  Window ke andar timestamps:
    [10:01:00, 10:01:10, 10:01:20, 10:01:30, 10:01:40]
  Count = 5

  5 < 5? NO → REJECT (429 Too Many Requests)
  Naya timestamp store NAHI karte

  Timestamps: [10:01:00, 10:01:10, 10:01:20, 10:01:30, 10:01:40]
  (unchanged)

  Note: Last 60 sec me already 5 requests ho chuki thi,
        aur koi timestamp window se bahar nahi gaya.
        Count 5 hi raha → REJECT.


### REQUEST 7 (user:101) : 10:02:10
────────────────────────────────────

  Window = [10:02:10 - 60s, 10:02:10]
         = [10:01:10, 10:02:10]

  Window ke andar timestamps:
    [10:01:10, 10:01:20, 10:01:30, 10:01:40]

  Count = 4 < 5 → ALLOW
  Naya timestamp add karo: 10:02:10

  Timestamps: [10:01:10, 10:01:20, 10:01:30, 10:01:40, 10:02:10]
  Count = 5 (limit reached again)

  Note: 10:01:00 ab window se bahar ho gaya
        (kyunki 10:01:10 se pehle tha)
        Isliye count 5 se 4 ho gaya — aur naya request ALLOW ho gaya.
        Naya timestamp add hone ke baad count dubara 5 ho gaya.



### NOTE :
-----------

Sliding Window Log me ZSET (Redis Sorted Set) ki har user ki key par TTL
isliye lagate hain kyunki agar user gayab ho jaaye, to uska ZSET Redis me
permanently padi rahegi — memory leak.

TTL (EXPIRE) sirf user ki PEHLI request pe lagate hain — jab Redis ZSET me
us user ki koi entry nahi hoti. Subsequent requests pe EXPIRE nahi lagate —
simplicity aur efficiency ke liye i.e extra Redis call bachane ke liye
sirf pehli request pe lagate hain.




### QUESTION : KAHAN USE KARTE HAIN ?
--------------------------------------

Sliding Window Log sirf tab use karte hain jab exact count mandatory ho,
volume kam ho (per-user 5-10 req/min), aur memory constraint na ho.

Ye memory heavy hai — har request ka timestamp Redis ZSET me store karta
hai. High-volume system me Redis memory blow up ho jaayega.

Isliye production me iska use selective hai — commonly nahi. Security-critical
endpoints pe use karte hain, jahan exact count chahiye aur boundary burst
acceptable nahi — jaise Login, Forgot Password, OTP.




3. SLIDING WINDOW COUNTER
---------------------------

## QUESTION : KYUN ZAROORAT PADI ? 
------------------------------------

Fixed Window me boundary burst problem hai — window reset hone pe attacker 2x limit bypass kar sakta hai. 
Sliding Window Log ne ye solve kiya, har request ka timestamp store karke. Lekin ye memory aur CPU heavy hai,
high volume me Redis blow up ho jaata hai. Isliye Sliding Window Counter use karte hain.


## QUESTION : SLIDING WINDOW COUNTER KAISE KAAM KARTA HAI ? 
-------------------------------------------------------------

1: Sliding Window Counter mein time ko Fixed Window Counter ki tarah fixed-size windows mein divide kiya jaata hai.

2: Hum har user ke liye do counters maintain karte hain:
    a) Previous Window Count: Previous fixed window mein user ki total requests ka count.
    b) Current Window Count: Current fixed window ke start se lekar ab tak user ki total requests ka count.

   Current Window = Woh fixed window jismein user ki current request aati hai. 
                   Har request kisi na kisi fixed window ka part hoti hai, jaise 
                   humne Fixed Window Counter mein dekha tha.

3: Ab hum user ki current request ke liye actual sliding window calculate karte hain.
   Sliding Window = [Current Time - Window Size, Current Time]

4: Ab hum check karte hain ki actual sliding window ka kitna portion previous fixed window mein lie karta hai.

    ** Previous Window Weight = Previous Window ka Sliding Window mein included time / Total Window Size
    ** Previous Weighted Count = Previous Window Count × Previous Window Weight

5: Ab hum estimate karte hain ki actual sliding window ke andar user ki total kitni requests aayi hain.
     ** Estimated Count = Previous Weighted Count + Current Window Count

6: Ab hum check karte hain:
     ** Estimated Count + 1 <= Allowed Limit ?

   - Agar condition satisfy hoti hai, toh request ALLOW kar denge aur Current Window Counter increment kar denge.
   - Agar condition satisfy nahi hoti, toh request REJECT kar denge aur 429 Too Many Requests response return karenge.


### IMPORTANT :
----------------

Sliding Window Counter ka calculation APPROXIMATE hota hai, exact nahi, kyunki hum previous window
mein har request ka individual timestamp store nahi karte. Hum sirf previous window ka total count
maintain karte hain aur time ke according uska weighted portion estimate karte hain.


## EXAMPLE :
-------------

Maan lo:

- User ID     : 101
- Rate Limit  : 100 requests/hour/user
- Window Size : 1 hour = 3,600 seconds


### STEP 1: ACTUAL SLIDING WINDOW CALCULATE KARNA
---------------------------------------------------

Maan lo user (user_id = 101) ne 11:30 AM par request ki
(midnight se 41,400 seconds).

Ye request 11:00 AM – 12:00 PM wale fixed window mein aayegi.

    Current Fixed Window  : 11:00 AM – 12:00 PM
    Previous Fixed Window : 10:00 AM – 11:00 AM

Actual Sliding Window calculate karenge:

    Sliding Window = [Current Time - Window Size, Current Time]
                   = [11:30 AM - 1 hour, 11:30 AM]
                   = 10:30 AM – 11:30 AM


### STEP 2: PREVIOUS WINDOW KA RELEVANT PORTION CALCULATE KARNA
-----------------------------------------------------------------

    Previous Window : 10:00 AM – 11:00 AM
    Current Window  : 11:00 AM – 12:00 PM
    Sliding Window  : 10:30 AM – 11:30 AM

Actual sliding window ka 50% portion previous fixed window mein
aur 50% portion current fixed window mein aata hai.


### STEP 3: PREVIOUS FIXED WINDOW KA WEIGHTED COUNT CALCULATE KARNA
---------------------------------------------------------------------

- Hume previous fixed window mein user ki total requests ka count pata hai.
  Maan lo previous fixed window mein 80 requests aayi thi.

- Actual sliding window mein previous window ka sirf 50% portion
  included hai. Isliye previous count ka 50% consider karenge.
  Ise Previous Weighted Count kehte hain.

    Previous Window Weight
        = Previous Window ka Sliding Window mein included time / Total Window Size
        = 30 minutes / 60 minutes
        = 0.5 (50%)

    Previous Weighted Count
        = Previous Window Count × Previous Window Weight
        = 80 × 0.5
        = 40 requests


### STEP 4: ESTIMATED COUNT CALCULATE KARNA
---------------------------------------------

- Maan lo current fixed window mein, yaani 11:00 AM se 11:30 AM tak,
  user ki 40 requests aa chuki hain.

    Estimated Count
        = Previous Weighted Count + Current Window Count
        = 40 + 40
        = 80 requests


### STEP 5: NEW REQUEST ALLOW YA REJECT KARNA
-----------------------------------------------

Rate Limit = 100 requests/hour/user.

Nayi request ko include karne ke liye check karenge:

    Estimated Count + 1 <= Allowed Limit
    80 + 1             <= 100
    81                 <= 100   →  TRUE ✅

Condition satisfy hoti hai, isliye request ALLOW kar denge
aur Current Window ka Counter increment kar denge.

Agar new request include karne par estimated count rate limit
exceed karta, toh request REJECT kar dete aur
429 Too Many Requests response return karte.



## QUESTION : SLIDING WINDOW COUNTER KAISE IMPLEMENT KARTE HAI ?
------------------------------------------------------------------

Sliding Window Counter bhi Fixed Window Counter ki tarah
similar way mein Redis use karke implement karte hain.

Do differences hain:

1. COUNTERS

    Fixed Window Counter    → 1 counter  (current window ka)
    Sliding Window Counter  → 2 counters (current + previous window)

2. TTL

    Fixed Window Counter    → TTL = 1 × windowSize
    Sliding Window Counter  → TTL = 2 × windowSize

Baaki sab same hai:

    - Window ID calculation same
    - Key format same
    - INCR pattern same


### QUESTION : TTL 2x KYUN RAKHTE HAIN ?
------------------------------------------

Kyunki immediate next window me jab hum Redis lookup karenge,
to hume immediate previous window ka counter bhi chahiye hoga.

Agar TTL = 1 × windowSize set kiya hota,
to us user ki immediate previous window ki key tab tak expire ho chuki hoti
jab hum immediate next window ke liye lookup karte.

Isliye TTL = 2 × windowSize rakhna zaroori hai.



## QUESTION : SLIDING WINDOW COUNTER KAB USE KARNA HAI ?
----------------------------------------------------------

Sliding Window Counter tab use karte hain jab approximate count chalega — jaise general API rate limiting
jahan thoda overcount ya undercount se koi farak nahi padta.



## QUESTION : SLIDING WINDOW COUNTER KI LIMITATIONS KYA HAI ?
--------------------------------------------------------------

- Ye approximate hai, exact nahi. Kyunki hum previous window ka distribution uniform assume karte hain,
  jisse thoda overcount hota hai — safe direction me, but galat bhi hai.

- Boundary burst 100% solve nahi hoti. Fixed Window se better hai (95% solve), but guarantee nahi hai,
  extreme cases me thoda leak reh jaata hai.




4. TOKEN BUCKET
----------------

## QUESTION : TOKEN BUCKET KAISE KAAM KARTA HAI ?
---------------------------------------------------

1: Token Bucket me hum har user ke liye ek virtual bucket maintain karte hain jisme tokens hote hain.

3: Har incoming user request ko process karne ke liye 1 token chahiye.

4: Isme 3 important cheezein hoti hain:

   a) Bucket Capacity:
      - Bucket me maximum kitne tokens store ho sakte hain.
      - Example: Capacity = 100 → ek saath max 100 requests allow hongi.

   b) Available Tokens:
      - Current time par bucket me kitne tokens available hain.

   c) Refill Rate:
      - Bucket me kitne time me kitne naye tokens add honge.
      - Example: Refill Rate = 10 tokens/sec, har second me 10 naye tokens add honge.


      ## QUESTION : TOKEN REFILL KAISE HOTA HAI?
      --------------------------------------------

       APPROACH 1: PERIODIC REFILL
       ----------------------------
       - Har X seconds me tokens add karo. Iske liye ek background job chahiye jo
         scheduled time par certain naye tokens ko add kare.

       APPROACH 2: LAZY REFILL
       ------------------------
       - Jab request aati hai, tab calculate karo ki last refill ke baad kitna time guzra.
         Us elapsed time ke basis par tokens add karo. Isme koi background job nahi chahiye.
       - Ye approach production me use hoti hai.
       - Formula:
         Elapsed Time               = Current Time - Last Refill Time
         Tokens To Add (max)        = Elapsed Time × Refill Rate
         Tokens To Add (final)      = min(Bucket Capacity, Available Tokens + Tokens To Add (max))

         Isse ensure hota hai ki bucket ki capacity exceed na ho.


5: Agar bucket me token available hai, toh request ALLOW kar denge aur 1 token consume ho jayega.

6: Agar bucket me token available nahi hai, toh request REJECT kar denge aur
   429 Too Many Requests response return karenge.



## QUESTION : TOKEN BUCKET KI ZAROORAT PADI ?
------------------------------------------------

- Fixed Window, Sliding Window Log, Sliding Window Counter — teeno request-count-based hain.

- Ye teeno normal burst allow nahi karte.

  Example:
  Limit = 10 req/min
  → In teeno me 1 minute window me 10 se zyada requests allow nahi hongi.

- Lekin real-world me legitimate burst allow karna hota hai.

  Example:
  User ne 5 minute tak koi request nahi bheji, phir ek saath 20 requests bheji.
  → Strict count-based approach ise reject kar degi, chahe ye legitimate ho.

- Token Bucket ye problem solve karta hai — Controlled burst allow karta hai.

  Example:
  - Bucket Capacity = 100 tokens
  - Refill Rate = 10 tokens/min

  User ne 5 min tak kuch nahi bheja → bucket me 100 tokens available hain.

  Ab user 1 min me 100 requests bhejta hai:
  → Sabhi 100 requests ALLOW ho jayengi (bucket me 100 tokens the).

  Dhyaan do — limit 10 req/min thi, phir bhi 100 requests allow hui.
  Isi ko controlled burst kehte hain.

  Lekin ye controlled hai:
  Agar wahi user 200 requests bhejta,
  → pehli 100 ALLOW (tokens the),
  → next 100 REJECT (bucket empty).

  Matlab burst allow hota hai — but bucket capacity tak hi.
  Isse backend par sudden unlimited load nahi padta.



## QUESTION : DISTRIBUTED SYSTEM ME TOKEN BUCKET KAISE IMPLEMENT KARTE HAIN?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Distributed system me Token Bucket ko Redis ki help se implement karte hain,
  taaki sabhi application instances ek shared bucket state use karein.

- Client HTTP request bhejta hai, jo Load Balancer tak pahunchti hai.
  Load Balancer request ko kisi ek Application Instance par route karta hai.

- Application me Bucket Capacity aur Refill Rate configured hote hain.
  Example: Bucket Capacity = 5 tokens, Refill Rate = 1 token/second.

- Application Instance user ki identity (jaise user_id = 101) ke basis par
  Redis key construct karta hai.

  KEY FORMAT:

  ratelimit:token_bucket:{entity}:{entity_id}

  Example:

  ratelimit:token_bucket:user:101

  Is key ke through application user 101 ki bucket identify karegi.

  Har user ki bucket state Redis mein separately store hoti hai, aur us user
  ki unique Redis key ke through identify ki jaati hai.

  Har key ke against Redis me do values store hoti hain:

  - tokens       → currently available tokens
  - last_refill  → last refill timestamp

- Application Redis se check karti hai ki ye key already exist karti hai ya nahi.

  GET ratelimit:token_bucket:user:101


- FIRST REQUEST: Agar Redis key exist nahi karti (GET ka response nil),
  toh Application:

     1: Tokens ko Bucket Capacity ke equal set karegi.
     2: Last Refill Timestamp ko current time par set karegi.
     3: Ek token consume karegi.
     4: Key ke liye TTL set kiya ja sakta hai, taaki inactive users ki Redis keys 
        automatically expire ho jaayein aur unnecessary keys memory occupy na karein. 
        TTL ko har subsequent request par update karna optional hai.
     5: Updated bucket state Redis me save karegi.
     6: Request ALLOW kar degi.


- NEXT REQUESTS: Application:

     1: Last refill ke baad elapsed time ke basis par tokens refill calculate
        karti hai, aur refill karti hai — lekin bucket capacity se zyada
        tokens nahi ho sakte.

        Note: Refill sirf tab hoga jab elapsed time, refill rate ki window
        ko cross kar chuka ho.
        Example: Agar limit 100 tokens/hour hai aur elapsed time 1 hour se
        kam hai, to koi token refill nahi hoga.

     2: Agar token available hai → Application ek token consume karke updated 
        bucket state Redis mein save karegi. Agar required ho, toh key ka TTL
        bhi update karegi. Uske baad request ALLOW ho jaayegi.

     3: Agar token available nahi hai → request REJECT hogi with
        429 Too Many Requests.



## QUESTION : TOKEN BUCKET KAB USE KARNA HAI ?
------------------------------------------------

Token Bucket wahan use karo jahan legitimate burst aata hai, lekin sustained rate control karna hai,
matlab traffic kabhi kam, kabhi ek saath bahut saari requests. Un bursts ko reject nahi karna hai,
but overall ek cap maintain rahe taaki backend overload na ho — aur cap cross hone par requests reject ho jaayein.
Example:

1. Payment / Banking APIs 
----------------------------

Merchant normal din me configured rate limit ke andar rehta hai — i.e. wo limited number of payments hi karta hai,
isliye uski bucket full rehti hai. Lekin bulk payout (salary, vendor payment, refunds) ke time ek saath hundreds of
requests aati hain jo configured limit cross kar jaati hain. Token Bucket me jama tokens ek saath saari requests allow 
kar dete hain — burst accommodate ho jaata hai. Cap (bucket capacity) cross hone par requests reject ho jaati hain, 
jisse backend overload nahi hota.


2. Flash Sale / Event Traffic
-------------------------------
Sale se pehle traffic bahut kam hota hai — isliye bucket full refill ho jaati hai. Sale start hote hi seconds me spike 
aa jaata hai. Strict rate limiting lagayen to genuine users bhi block ho jaayenge. Token Bucket me jama tokens ek saath 
bahut saari requests allow kar dete hain — spike accommodate ho jaata hai, but cap (bucket capacity) tak hi. Cap cross 
hone par requests reject ho jaati hain, jisse backend crash nahi hota.



### QUESTION : TOKEN BUCKET KI MAJOR LIMITATION KYA HAI ?
----------------------------------------------------------

Token Bucket ki sabse badi limitation ye hai ki wo burst ko fully allow karta hai. 
Jab bucket full hota hai, to ek saath poori capacity jitni requests allow ho jaati 
hain — jaise capacity 100 hai to 100 requests ek hi second me pass ho jaayengi. 
Isse backend par sudden load spike aa jaata hai.
Legitimate burst ke liye ye achha hai, lekin agar attacker jaan-bujh kar bucket full karke burst kare,
to backend overload ho sakta hai. Isliye production me capacity chhoti rakhte hain,
taaki burst limited rahe aur backend usse safely handle kar sake.




5: LEAKY BUCKET
-----------------

- Leaky Bucket algorithm incoming requests ko ek FIXED RATE par process karta hai,
  Requests chahe ek saath kitni bhi tezi se aayein, unhe downstream processing system
  tak controlled rate par bheja jaata hai.
- Isme hum ek bucket consider karte hain jiske niche ek hole hota hai.
- Bucket ki BUCKET CAPACITY define karti hai ki maximum kitni requests bucket
  mein wait kar sakti hain.
     Example: Capacity = 100 → Maximum 100 requests queue mein wait kar sakti hain.
- Bucket se requests ko ek CONTROLLED RATE par downstream processing system tak
  bheja jaata hai. Is rate ko LEAK RATE kehte hain.
     Example: Leak Rate = 2 requests/second → Bucket se har second 2 requests
     process ki jaati hain.
- Agar bucket full ho jaaye, toh new incoming requests ko REJECT kar diya jaata hai.



## QUESTION : LEAKY BUCKET KO IMPLEMENT KAISE KARTE HAIN ?
----------------------------------------------------------

1: SINGLE INSTANCE
---------------------

- Agar service ka sirf ek instance hai, toh Leaky Bucket implement karne ke liye application ke andar
  fixed-capacity FIFO queue maintain kar sakte hain. Java mein iske liye ArrayBlockingQueue use karenge,
  jisme incoming requests FIFO order mein store hoti hain.

- Request ko queue mein insert karne ke liye offer() method use karenge, kyunki yeh non-blocking operation hai.
   offer() → Queue full hone par turant false return karta hai; producer thread block nahi hota.
   put() → Queue full hone par space available hone tak producer thread block ho jaata hai.

   ### IMPORTANT :
   -----------------
   ArrayBlockingQueue khud non-blocking queue nahi hai. Yeh ek bounded, thread-safe blocking queue hai.
   Lekin offer() method use karne par producer thread queue full hone ki wajah se block nahi hota.

- Agar queue full ho aur nayi request aaye, toh offer() false return karega aur application us request 
  ko reject kar deti hai.

- Ek scheduler fixed interval pe (jaise har 1 second) kisi job ko invoke karta hai.
  Job elapsed time aur defined leak rate ke basis par calculate karta hai ki
  queue se kitni requests nikaal kar process karni hain.

- EXAMPLE :

  Leak Rate = 10 requests per minute

  1 minute = 60 seconds
  Toh 60 seconds me 10 requests process karni hain.

  Matlab approximately har 6 seconds me 1 request process honi chahiye.


  TIMELINE :

  t=1s    → scheduler chala, job invoke → process 0 request
  t=2s    → scheduler chala, job invoke → process 0 request
  t=3s    → scheduler chala, job invoke → process 0 request
  t=4s    → scheduler chala, job invoke → process 0 request
  t=5s    → scheduler chala, job invoke → process 0 request
  t=6s    → scheduler chala, job invoke → process 1 request ✅
  t=7s    → scheduler chala, job invoke → process 0 request
  t=8s    → scheduler chala, job invoke → process 0 request
  ...

  t=60s   → total 10 requests process ho chuki hongi ✅


2: DISTRIBUTED SYSTEM (MULTIPLE INSTANCE)
------------------------------------------

Shared queue maintain karne ke liye RabbitMQ ya Kafka jaise message brokers
use kiye ja sakte hain.

Service-specific LB requests ko application instances ke beech distribute karta
hai. Ye Application instances (Producers) incoming requests ko shared queue me
publish karte hain. Consumer(s) us queue ko listen/subscribe karte hain aur
fixed leak rate pe messages consume karte hain.

Consumer do tarah se process kar sakta hai:
   a) Khud process kare
   b) Downstream service ko delegate kare

Agar downstream service ko bhej rahe hain, to leak rate us downstream service
ki processing speed aur capacity dekh kar set karna hota hai. Warna overall
load downstream service par overload ho jaayega.

Important:
   - Queue full ho → publish fail → 429 return


## QUESTION: LEAKY BUCKET KI MAJOR LIMITATIONS KYA HAIN?
------------------------------------------------------------

1. Requests queue mein wait karti hain, jiski wajah se response time increase ho sakta hai. 
   Ye real-time APIs ke liye problem ho sakta hai, jahan low response time chahiye hota hai.

2. Agar bucket full ho jaaye, toh additional requests reject ho jaati hain, chahe traffic spike temporary hi kyon na ho.


## QUESTION: LEAKY BUCKET KA USE KAHAN HOTA HAI?
---------------------------------------------------

1. Downstream Service Load Management:
  -  Downstream service tak requests ko uski request-processing capacity ke according controlled rate par bhejne ke liye.
  -  Example: Agar Payment Service sustainably 100 requests/second process kar sakti hai, toh requests ko approximately 100 requests/second ki rate se bhejna.

2. Third-Party API Rate Control:
  - Third-party API ko requests uski allowed rate ke according bhejna, taaki rate limit exceed na ho.





