# QUESTION : BACKPRESSURE KYA HAI ?
======================================

- Backpressure ek aisa mechanism hai jisme downstream service, apni processing capacity se zyada load aa jaane par, 
  upstream service ko signal bhejti hai ki wo apni request bhejne ki speed kam kar de ya temporarily rok de. 
  Isse downstream service overload hone se bachti hai.
  
- Maan lo Order Service, Payment Service ko 1,000 payment requests/sec bhej rahi hai, lekin Payment Service ki processing 
  capacity sirf 500 requests/sec hai. Aise mein Payment Service, backpressure ke through Order Service ko requests bhejne 
  ki speed kam karne ka signal de sakti hai, taaki Payment Service overload na ho. **Lekin yeh tabhi possible hai jab dono 
  services ke beech communication mechanism backpressure support karta ho.


## QUESTION: BACKPRESSURE KO AAM TAUR PAR KAISE IMPLEMENT KARTE HAIN? — BOUNDED NON BLOCKING QUEUE + REJECTION + RETRY-AFTER SIGNAL
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Service/applciation mein ek fixed-size FIFO queue maintain ki jaati hai, jaise 1,000 requests ki,
jisme incoming requests temporarily buffer hoti hain. Jab queue full ho jaati hai, toh 
nayi request ko turant reject karke `503 Service Unavailable` ya situation ke according
`429 Too Many Requests` response diya ja sakta hai. Saath mein optional `Retry-After: 30` 
header bhej sakte hain, jo client/producer ko 30 seconds baad retry karne ka signal deta hai,
taaki service ko recover hone ka time mil sake.

Queue full hone par request reject karne ke saath, agar communication mechanism support karta hai,
toh overloaded downstream service upstream service ko signal deti hai ki woh requests bhejne ki speed
kam kare ya temporarily requests bhejna rok de. Isse downstream service overload hone se bachti hai aur 
recover karne ka time milta hai.

Agar request queue full hone ki wajah se definitely reject hui hai, toh woh process nahi hui;
isliye us attempt se duplicate processing ka risk nahi hai. Lekin payment jaise critical operations
mein retries ke dauran same Idempotency-Key use karna general safety practice hai.

Java mein bounded queue ke liye 'ArrayBlockingQueue' use kar sakte hain. Request insert karne ke liye offer()
method use karenge, kyunki yeh queue full hone par producer thread ko block nahi karta.

* put() — queue full hone par thread wait karta hai.
* offer() — queue full hone par turant false return karta hai.

Agar offer() false return kare, toh application request reject karke appropriate HTTP error response bhej sakti hai.
Isse system fail-fast karta hai aur producer thread unnecessarily block nahi hota.

BlockingQueue<PaymentRequest> queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(1000);

boolean accepted = queue.offer(paymentRequest);

if (!accepted) {
    // Reject request with 503 Service Unavailable
}


### IMPORTANT : 
---------------
ArrayBlockingQueue application instance ki in-memory queue hoti hai. 
Agar application ki 4 instances hain, toh har instance ki apni separate queue hoti  hai. 




======================================
# QUESTION : LOAD SHEDDING KYA HAI ?
======================================

Load Shedding ek mechanism hai jisme jab system par incoming traffic uski processing capacity
se zyada ho jaata hai, toh system kuch requests ko intentionally reject kar deta hai, taaki 
poora system overload hokar fail na ho jaaye. Iska main goal hai system ko stable rakhna aur
critical requests ko process karte rehna.


## QUESTION: LOAD SHEDDING KAISE IMPLEMENT KARTE HAIN?
---------------------------------------------------------

1. CONCURRENCY LIMIT 
----------------------

 Maan lo ek service ek time par maximum 500 requests concurrently process kar sakti hai, 
 lekin upstream se 1,000 requests concurrently aa rahi hain. Agar saari 500 processing slots
 occupied hain, toh additional requests reject kar di jaati hain. Isko implement karne ke 
 liye Semaphore ya concurrency limiter use kar sakte hain.

 ### Implementation using Java Semaphore:

 Semaphore semaphore = new Semaphore(500); // maximum 500 concurrent requests ko permit karega.

 if (!semaphore.tryAcquire()) { // Request aane par tryAcquire() se permit acquire karo.
    // Permit available nahi hai -> Reject request: 503 Service Unavailable
    return;
 }

 try {
    processPayment();
 } finally {
    // Processing complete hone par release() se permit release karo.
    semaphore.release();
 }

 ## NOTE :
 ----------
 Semaphore(500) ek application instance mein 500 concurrent requests limit karega. 
 Agar service ki 4 instances hain, toh har instance ka separate semaphore hone par
 total limit 2,000 tak ho sakti hai.



2. QUEUE BASED 
----------------

Service/application mein ek fixed-size FIFO queue maintain ki jaati hai, jaise 1,000 requests ki, 
jisme incoming requests temporarily buffer hoti hain. 

- Queue mein space hai → request accept karke queue mein add kar do.
- Queue full hai → new request ko reject kar do aur appropriate HTTP response, jaise 503 Service Unavailable, return kar do.

Java mein bounded queue ke liye ArrayBlockingQueue use kar sakte hain. offer() method 
queue full hone par producer thread ko block kiye bina false return karta hai.

### NOTE: 
----------
Load Shedding aur Backpressure ko ek saath implement bhi kiya ja sakta hai. Jaise queue full hone par
service excess requests ko reject kare aur saath hi upstream ko requests ki speed kam karne ka signal bhi de.



3. PRIORITY BASED 
------------------

Is approach mein application mein request priorities define ki jaati hain aur system overload hone
par low-priority requests ko reject kiya jaata hai, taaki critical requests ke liye resources available rahein.

Maan lo ek e-commerce application mein:

- Payment requests: high priority
- Order status refresh: medium priority
- Recommendations: low priority

System overloaded hone par recommendations requests ko reject kar deta hai,
taaki critical payment requests ke liye resources available rahein.




